      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"Access Denied\" error is likely because the Lambda function is trying to write to the root of the bucket (`prod-logs-storage-99`) instead of the specific prefix you defined (`prod-logs-storage-99/*`). While your policy looks correct on paper, AWS S3 permissions are notoriously picky, and a tiny mismatch in the ARN path can cause a complete blockage.\n\nHere is the likely culprit: **The Lambda function's execution role might be trying to access the bucket root, or the bucket policy has a condition that restricts the key name.**\n\nTo fix this, ensure your IAM policy explicitly allows `s3:PutObject` on the specific prefix `arn:aws:s3:::prod-logs-storage-99/*`. If you are using a bucket policy to allow Lambda access, make sure it includes the `aws:SourceArn` condition to explicitly identify the Lambda execution role.\n\n**Why this happens:**\nS3 is a flat namespace, but your policy is trying to restrict it to a sub-path. If the Lambda function is configured to write to the root (e.g., `arn:aws:s3:::prod-logs-storage-99/`), the",
